[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. The reports in it tell a
different story. They were merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and nobody had to read the body. Read the bodies, and three problems come out, each with its own
fix. The parser fix that is queued closes only the first.

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the
  day first, so their dates come out flipped: "Booked for 2 March. The invite says 3 February."
- **`hint`**: tenants on `booking-v3` show the hint `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. Their
  people type what the hint says, and the form reads it the tenant's way. Same flip, different
  bug: fixing the parser leaves it in place. Only reports sent through the form.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by exactly one day, not a month. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
  Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." This looks like a time zone, not a parser. The
  desk spotted these in its notes, but the cause table files them under `parser` by tenant,
  so they disappear into the biggest row.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not known. Two of its three problems have no ticket and no fix. Each now has its own
ticket, linked in the table.
</Callout>

## How #4471 reads today

The desk's own table, for comparison. It counts the one-day moves under `parser` and footnotes
them.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why the merge hid it

- Subject lines don't name the cause. The same subject ("Date is wrong") covers all three.
- Intake tags guess from the reporter's browser, not the tenant. The "tagged as" column above
  shows how often they disagree with the cause.
- One ticket with a count reads as one bug. A count says how loud, not how many.

## Next

- Split by cause, not subject, before a ticket is marked known: run the [triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe).
- Read a sample of bodies in every big ticket. A handful of reports was enough to show a third bug.

